text.p:
We got into Oxford upon ye 16th Mr Musgrave & Mr Waugh (who had preached in York Minster as he came up) were before us;
& Mr Browham and his Brother came the last night, & this Afternoon the Lawyer went for London, & ye Tutor going as far as Tettsworth with him. We had a good journey, and the Dr was very glad to see me here, and more troubled than either you, or I, for ye late disappointment. It's whispered here that there's money in ye case. I wish Sr you would try your interest with Sr Christopher, & Sr John who may this winter find opportunities to oblige you. I desire you would write to ye Earl of Thanet as soon as you can, for I find others here upon ye same design; if Sr he would admit me titular Chaplain, till I might be more able to serve him, it would make him remember me the better. Sr Joseph Williamson is a great friend of yours. I wish you would try him, he is a public spirited man, and so may be readier to oblige you then others; in ye interim I shall make my function my business, & hope you will hear shortly of my being in the Pulpit. I am resolved to preach and live as well as I may. I fear Sr what you heard of my Brothers may be too true; upon their
promise of more regularity, I have paid a tick for them, to their Chandler of 13s & 10d & they have another at an Ale-house of 1£ - 3s; the former is for Brandy, Pips & Tobacco, by this you may judge. I shall give you Sr an exact account how they live this winter, & so desiring you would be pleased to give us your blessing, I rest
